Q: Why does the child theme CSS not override the parent theme header styles? A: <p>The child theme is loading correctly. The issue is CSS specificity, not the enqueue setup. Most modern WordPress themes do not style the header using the plain <code>header</code> selector. Instead, they use more specific selectors such as:</p>
<pre><code>header.site-header { }
.site-header { }
#masthead { }
header.header { }
</code></pre>
<p>In CSS, more specific selectors override less specific ones, even if the less specific rule is loaded later. To override the parent theme’s styles, use the exact selector defined in the parent theme.<br />
For example:</p>
<pre><code>.site-header {
    background-color: blue;
}

/* or */

#masthead {
    background-color: blue;
}
</code></pre>
<p>Using <code>!important</code> can force the override, but it should be avoided unless absolutely necessary.</p>

Q: Issue with sending data over WebSocket using encryption/certificate A: <p>This is a common issue with Unity WebGL and WSS connections. The problem occurs because Unity WebGL uses the browser's WebSocket implementation, which has stricter security requirements for WSS connections compared to native implementations.</p>
<p>Here are the most likely causes and solutions:</p>
<h2>1. Certificate Issues (Most Common)</h2>
<p><strong>Problem</strong> : Self-signed certificates or certificates not trusted by the browser cause silent failures after the initial handshake.</p>
<p><strong>Solution</strong> : Test your certificate setup:</p>
<pre><code>// Add this to your Node.js server for testing
app.get('/test', (req, res) =&gt; {
  res.send('HTTPS is working!');
});
</code></pre>
<p>Then visit</p>
<pre><code>https://your-server:55555/test
</code></pre>
<p>in the same browser you'll use for WebGL. If you get certificate warnings, that's your issue.</p>
<p><strong>Fix options</strong> :</p>
<ul>
<li><p>Use a proper SSL certificate from a trusted CA (Let's Encrypt is free)</p>
</li>
<li><p>For development, add your self-signed cert to the browser's trusted certificates</p>
</li>
<li><p>Test locally with</p>
<pre><code>localhost
</code></pre>
<p>instead of IP addresses (browsers are more lenient)</p>
</li>
</ul>
<h2>2. Mixed Content Policy</h2>
<p><strong>Problem</strong> : If your WebGL app is served over HTTP but tries to connect via WSS, browsers block it.</p>
<p><strong>Solution</strong> : Ensure your WebGL app is served over HTTPS when using WSS connections.</p>
<h2>3. Unity WebGL WebSocket Limitations</h2>
<p><strong>Problem</strong> : Unity WebGL doesn't handle certain WebSocket features the same way as native builds.</p>
